{"term":"Fleischeslust","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"lust","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The lust of the flesh; carnal desire framed in religious or moral terms as the base appetite of the body opposing the spirit.","english_term":"carnal lust","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Compound of Fleisch (flesh) + Lust (desire/pleasure); common in theological and literary German to denote sinful carnal appetite."}
{"term":"Fleischlich","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Carnal, fleshly; of or relating to the body and its desires, especially sexual appetite, as opposed to the spiritual.","english_term":"carnal","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Adjective; appears in fixed phrases like 'fleischliche Lust' (carnal lust) and 'fleischliche Begierde' (carnal desire); still recognizable from religious and literary contexts."}
{"term":"Unzüchtig","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"lewdness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lewd, unchaste, or indecent; sexually immoral or violating norms of public decency.","english_term":"lewd","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Former standard term in German criminal law (Unzuchtsdelikte = indecency offenses); largely replaced in modern usage by 'obszön' or 'unanständig'."}
{"term":"Sittlichkeitsdelikt","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"legal","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An offense against public morality or sexual decency; a minor sex crime or indecency offense as classified in the older German penal framework.","english_term":"indecency offense","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Used in pre-reform German criminal law; the broader category encompassing exhibitionism, public indecency, and lesser sexual offenses. Now superseded by more specific statutory terminology."}
{"term":"Sittlichkeitsverbrechen","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"legal","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A serious crime against morality or sexual decency; a felony-level sex crime in the older German legal classification, more severe than a Sittlichkeitsdelikt.","english_term":"sex crime","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound distinguishing a serious crime (Verbrechen) from a minor offense (Delikt); both terms appear in pre-reform German criminal codes to classify sexual offenses by severity."}
{"term":"Lüsternheit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":22,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lasciviousness, lustfulness; the quality of being driven by lascivious sexual desire in a morally reproachable or licentious way.","english_term":"lasciviousness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Noun form of 'lüstern' (lustful, lascivious); archaic in everyday speech but still encountered in literary and moral-philosophical texts."}
{"term":"Gemächt","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"penis","concept":"male_genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":22,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The male genitalia; an archaic German literary term for the male sex organs, etymologically related to 'machen' (to make/fashion), implying that which is endowed by nature.","english_term":"male member","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily encountered in Early New High German and 16th–18th century texts; almost entirely displaced by modern clinical and colloquial terms. Low recognition even among educated speakers today."}
{"term":"Schamteile","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"genitals","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The genitals; literally 'parts of shame,' an archaic German euphemism for the external sex organs used to avoid direct naming.","english_term":"private parts","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The 'Scham-' prefix (shame) reflects historical modesty norms; cf. Schamglieder, Schamhaar (pubic hair), Schamlippen (labia). Plural noun."}
{"term":"Schamglieder","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"genitals","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The genitals; literally 'members of shame,' a near-synonym of Schamteile with a slightly more clinical weight, used in older medical and religious German texts.","english_term":"pudenda","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Glieder' (members/limbs) gives it a quasi-anatomical register compared to Schamteile; both share the shaming 'Scham-' prefix."}
{"term":"Lustmord","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"lust_murder","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":65,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A sexually motivated murder; a killing in which sexual gratification is the primary motive; also a celebrated motif in Weimar-era German art and literature.","english_term":"lust murder","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of Lust (desire/pleasure) + Mord (murder). Gained cultural currency through Weimar-era art (Grosz, Dix, Wedekind) and infamous criminal trials (Haarmann, Kürten). 'Lust murder' exists in English criminology but lacks a registry slot; the German term carries specific cultural weight beyond the English gloss."}
{"term":"Lustmörder","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"lust_murder","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"archaic","crude":65,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person who commits a sexually motivated murder; a lust killer.","english_term":"lust killer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Agent noun of Lustmord; entered German cultural consciousness through the trials of Fritz Haarmann and Peter Kürten ('Vampir von Düsseldorf') in the 1920s–30s."}
{"term":"Schamlosigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shamelessness; a total absence of shame, especially in sexual or public behavior; brazen indecency.","english_term":"shamelessness","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Schamlos (shameless) + -igkeit; still in active modern German use, so recognition is higher than most items in this section; the moral/sexual connotation is more archaic than the general sense of brazenness."}
{"term":"Freudenknabe","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"kept_partner","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":38,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'boy of pleasure'; an archaic German term for a young male kept for sexual gratification, especially in pederastic contexts; a catamite.","english_term":"catamite","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The 'Freuden-' prefix echoes 'Freudenmädchen' (prostitute, lit. 'pleasure girl'); the 'Knabe' (boy) component signals an explicitly pederastic character not captured by 'kept_partner' alone. Extremely low recognition today."}
{"term":"Knabenliebe","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"ephebophilia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'love of boys'; an archaic/literary German term for pederasty, the erotic attraction of adult men to adolescent boys, often idealized through the lens of classical Greek culture.","english_term":"pederasty","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Distinct from clinical 'Pädophilie' in that Knabe implies a pubescent or adolescent male; the classical Greek framing aestheticizes what modern law classifies as child sexual abuse."}
{"term":"Päderastie","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"ephebophilia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":55,"technical":55,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Pederasty; the sexual relationship between an adult man and a pubescent or adolescent boy, borrowed from the ancient Greek practice.","english_term":"pederasty","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"A direct German transliteration of Greek/Latin 'paederastia'; more clinical in register than Knabenliebe; in historical usage often framed as a feature of Greek antiquity rather than a contemporary crime."}
{"term":"Hintertreppenroman","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"erotica","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":18,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'back-staircase novel'; a trashy, sensationalist pulp novel with scandalous romantic or erotic content; the German equivalent of a penny dreadful.","english_term":"pulp novel","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The 'Hintertreppe' (back stairs) evokes servants' entrances and clandestine reading — the kind of book hidden under the mattress. Not necessarily explicitly pornographic but reliably salacious; cf. Schundliteratur."}
{"term":"Schundliteratur","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"obscenity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Trash literature; low-quality, morally objectionable writing condemned for its sexual, violent, or otherwise corrupting content; the German equivalent of pulp smut.","english_term":"smut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Schund (trash/filth) + Literatur; central to early 20th-century German censorship debates ('Schmutz und Schund' campaigns). A moralistic umbrella term rather than a precise literary genre label."}
{"term":"Lustspiel","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"eroticism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":12,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'pleasure play'; primarily a theatrical comedy or light drama; in archaic erotic usage, an amorous game or playful sexual encounter.","english_term":"amorous play","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The theatrical meaning (comedy) strongly dominates modern usage; its erotic sense is archaic and subordinate. 'Lust' in early German meant pleasure broadly, not exclusively sexual desire; cf. Lustgarten (pleasure garden)."}
{"term":"Verführer","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"debaucher","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A seducer; a man who uses charm, flattery, or deception to persuade someone into sexual activity or moral corruption.","english_term":"seducer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also carries the theological sense of 'tempter' (the devil as Verführer); still in active modern use, hence higher recognition than most terms in this section. 'Debaucher' is the nearest registry slot but 'seducer' is the precise English gloss."}
{"term":"Verführerin","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"debaucher","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A seductress; a woman who uses charm, beauty, or deception to lead someone into sexual activity or moral corruption.","english_term":"seductress","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Feminine form of Verführer; in literary usage typically coded as a femme fatale archetype. Still in active modern use."}
{"term":"Verführung","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Seduction; the act of leading someone into sexual activity or moral transgression through charm, flattery, or deception.","english_term":"seduction","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Still very common in modern German; in older criminal law, 'Verführung zur Unzucht' (seduction to indecency) was a specific charge. 'Debauchery' is the nearest registry slot but 'seduction' is the precise English gloss."}
{"term":"Schmutzfinke","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"pervert","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A dirty, sexually perverted, or obscenity-obsessed person; a variant or dialectal form of Schmutzfink, literally 'dirty finch.'","english_term":"dirty pervert","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The -e suffix likely represents a dialectal or weak-declension variant of Schmutzfink; possibly used as a feminine form in some registers, though non-standard. See also Schmutzfink."}
{"term":"Schmutzfink","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"pervert","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'dirty finch'; a person with a penchant for sexual filth, crude humor, or pornographic material; a dirty pervert or smutty type.","english_term":"dirty pervert","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Schmutz (dirt/filth) + Fink (finch/sparrow); used both as a mild insult and affectionately depending on context; recognition is higher than most archaic terms in this section because it remains in colloquial use."}
{"term":"Kuppler","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"procuring","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A male procurer, panderer, or go-between; a man who arranges sexual encounters or facilitates prostitution for others.","english_term":"procurer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"From kuppeln (to couple/connect); in older German law, Kuppelei (procuring) was a criminal offense. The Kuppler arranged introductions between sex workers and clients."}
{"term":"Kupplerin","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":null,"concept":"procuring","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A female procuress or bawd; a woman who arranges sexual encounters or manages the supply of sex workers to clients.","english_term":"procuress","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Feminine form of Kuppler; historically associated with a discreet matchmaker for illicit assignations or a bawd managing sex workers. Kuppelei (the crime of procuring) was gender-neutral in application."}
